  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Present Subjunctive in Fixed Expressions",
      "content": "The subjunctive (subjuntivo) is a mood used to express wishes, doubts, emotions, and hypothetical situations. At A2.1, you'll mainly encounter it in fixed expressions — set phrases where the subjunctive is mandatory.\n\nWishes and blessings:\n- ¡Que tengas un buen día! = Have a good day!\n- ¡Que te vaya bien! = Good luck! (literally: may it go well for you)\n- ¡Que descanses! = Rest well!\n\nWith querer/esperar + que (when the subject changes):\n- Quiero que seas feliz. = I want you to be happy.\n- Espero que llueva. = I hope it rains.\n\nCommon fixed expressions:\n- Ojalá (que)... = I hope that... / God willing...\n- Es importante que... = It's important that...\n- Mejor que... = Better if...\n\nThe present subjunctive yo form is built by taking the yo present indicative form and switching the ending: -o → -e for -AR, -o → -a for -ER/-IR:\n- hablar → hablo → hable\n- comer → como → coma\n- vivir → vivo → viva",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "Infinitive",
          "Yo Present Indicative",
          "Present Subjunctive Yo"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "hablar",
            "hablo",
            "hable"
          ],
          [
            "comer",
            "como",
            "coma"
          ],
          [
            "vivir",
            "vivo",
            "viva"
          ],
          [
            "tener",
            "tengo",
            "tenga"
          ],
          [
            "estar",
            "estoy",
            "esté"
          ],
          [
            "ir",
            "voy",
            "vaya"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        "¡Que tengas un excelente día! (Have an excellent day!)",
        "Ojalá llueva mañana. (I hope it rains tomorrow.)",
        "Quiero que tú vengas a mi fiesta. (I want you to come to my party.)",
        "Espero que esté bien. (I hope he/she is okay.)"
      ],
      "aid_note": "Romanian uses a similar 'optative' mood for wishes: 'Să ai o zi bună!' = '¡Que tengas un buen día!' The 'să' particle marks the subjunctive optative. This makes the Spanish subjunctive much more natural for Romanian speakers than for English speakers."
    }
  ],
  "pronunciation": [],
  "exercises": [
    {
      "type": "exercise",
      "question": "Which of these uses the subjunctive correctly?",
      "options": [
        "Quiero que tú vienes.",
        "Ojalá llueva mañana.",
        "Espero que tú comes.",
        "Quiero que tú comes."
      ],
      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "'Ojalá llueva mañana' uses the subjunctive correctly. 'Ojalá' always triggers the subjunctive."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: '¡Que tengas ____ día!' (Have a ____ day!) — subjunctive wish for someone.",
      "correct": "bueno",
      "explanation": "'¡Que tengas un buen día!' uses the subjunctive 'tengas' (from tener) after 'que'."
    },
    {
      "type": "exercise",
      "question": "What is the subjunctive yo form of 'tener'?",
      "options": [
        "tengo",
        "tuviera",
        "tenga",
        "tenía"
      ],
      "correct": 2,
      "explanation": "'Tenga' is the present subjunctive yo form: take the yo indicative (tengo), drop the -o, add -a for -ER verbs."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: 'Espero que ____ (estar) bien.' (I hope he/she is okay.)",
      "correct": "esté",
      "explanation": "'Esté' is the subjunctive of estar for él/ella/usted: take yo present (estoy), drop -o, add -e: esté."
    },
    {
      "type": "fill-in-blank",
      "question": "Complete: 'Quiero que ____ (you) ____ (come) a mi fiesta.' (I want you to come to my party.)",
      "correct": "vengas",
      "explanation": "'Vengas' is the subjunctive of venir for tú. Irregular: venir → vengo → vengas."
    },
    {
      "type": "exercise",
      "question": "What triggers the subjunctive after 'ojalá'?",
      "options": [
        "No trigger",
        "The subjunctive mood",
        "The infinitive",
        "The indicative"
      ],
      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "'Ojalá' always triggers the subjunctive: Ojalá llueva, Ojalá venga, etc."
    }
  ]
